    "body": "Closes #7527\n\n`it += 1` (or `it = it + 1`) on an untyped variable holding a C++ iterator crashed the compiler during type inference. `BinopNode.result_type()` returns `None` for C++ class operands (unlike the unary operators, it doesn't look up the overloaded operator), and `infer_name_node_type()` passed that `None` on to `spanning_type()`.\n\nThis looks up the matching operator overload in `result_type()`, the same lookup `analyse_cpp_operation()` does, so the variable is inferred as the iterator type and the loop from the issue works. If no overload matches, `infer_name_node_type()` now falls back to a Python object like the entry-level inference already does, so you get `Cannot convert 'iterator' to Python object` instead of a crash.\n\nSide effect: an untyped variable assigned from a C++ binary operation (e.g. `y = s1 + s2` with `std::string`) is now inferred as the C++ result type instead of a Python object, the same as `y = s1`. The generated code for the existing C++ run tests doesn't change.\n",
    "closed_at": null,
